// Vitest Snapshot v1, https://vitest.dev/guide/snapshot.html

exports[`BridgeInfoPage rendered text is unchanged by the #1097 toolkit extraction > matches the frozen baseline for Linux 1`] = `
"BackProPulse BridgeConnect your radio hardware to PropulseDisconnectedws://127.0.0.1:9867Start the bridge server to connectSetup GuideWhat is the Bridge?The ProPulse Bridge is a lightweight server that runs on your computer alongside Propulse.It acts as a secure translator between the web application and your radio equipment — your transceiver, logging software, and digital mode programs.Because it runs only on localhost, your equipment is never exposed to the internet.Communication happens over WebSocket — a real-time, bidirectional protocol that lets Propulse and your radio talk to each other with millisecond latency.CapabilitiesCAT ControlControl your transceiver directly from the browser. Tune to a DX spot with one click, switch modes for digital or CW, activate PTT for transmit.Powered by Hamlib, supporting over 2,000 radio models from Icom, Yaesu, Kenwood, Elecraft, FlexRadio, and more.What this means: Hear a rare DX station on the cluster? One click and your radio is already on frequency.DX Cluster RelayReceive real-time DX spots from the worldwide cluster network directly in Propulse. Spots appear on the PropSphere map and band planner automatically.Connects to standard DX cluster nodes via telnet — works with any public node. No separate telnet client needed.What this means: See who's on the air right now, where they are on the globe, and jump to their frequency instantly.WSJT-X IntegrationSee FT8, FT4, and JT65 decodes from WSJT-X in real-time within Propulse. Auto-log QSOs when WSJT-X reports a completed contact.Listens on WSJT-X's standard UDP multicast port — zero configuration in most setups. Track which callsigns are being decoded on the waterfall.What this means: Your digital mode activity flows seamlessly into your Propulse logbook and map.Multi-Operator SyncCoordinate multiple operators during contest operations from different computers. Frequency locking prevents two operators from transmitting on the same frequency.QSO deconfliction avoids duplicate contacts. Shared notes and session state keep everyone in sync — all in real-time.What this means: Run a multi-op contest station with Propulse as your coordination hub.Live ConnectionDisconnectedTest ConnectionArchitectureThe bridge sits between your browser and hardware, routing messages over localhost.Propulse(Browser)Bridge Serverlocalhost:9867WebSocketHamlibrigctldDX ClusterTelnetWSJT-XUDP 2237Your RadioTransceiverMessage ProtocolAll messages use a JSON envelope format with type, id, timestamp, and payload fields.Security ModelBinds exclusively to localhost (127.0.0.1) — no remote connections acceptedCAT control commands cannot be issued from outside your computerContest data and QSO information never leave your local networkNeed remote access for multi-computer setups?ssh -L 9867:127.0.0.1:9867 user@contest-pcSSH tunnels forward the bridge port securely. Only use this with trusted machines and authenticated SSH keys.Setup GuidePick your platformSteps shown for Linux.WindowsmacOSLinux1Install the BridgeClone or download the Propulse repository, then install dependencies:cd bridge
npm installAlternatively, download propulse-bridge-x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu from GitHub Releases. For Raspberry Pi, use the aarch64 variant.2Start the ServerDevelopment mode (auto-reloads on changes):npm run devProduction mode:npm run build
npm start3Verify ConnectionLook for the green dot next to System Health in the Propulse header. It should show "Bridge: Connected."You can also use the "Test Connection" button above to verify.4Configure CAT Control (optional)Install Hamlib and start rigctld with your radio's model number and serial port:rigctld -m 3085 -r /dev/ttyUSB0 -s 38400On Linux, you may need to add your user to the dialout group for serial port access: sudo usermod -aG dialout $USER5Configure DX Cluster (optional)Add a cluster node address in the bridge configuration. Common public nodes include:# Example bridge config
cluster_host = "dxc.nc7j.com"
cluster_port = 7373
callsign = "YOUR_CALL"6Configure WSJT-X (optional)In WSJT-X, go to File → Settings → Reporting and enable "Accept UDP requests".The default UDP port is 2237. The bridge listens on this port automatically.TroubleshootingBridge shows 'Disconnected'Confirm the bridge server is running (npm run dev or the standalone binary).Check that port 9867 is not blocked by your firewall.Ensure the WebSocket URL matches (ws://127.0.0.1:9867).Connection keeps droppingSome antivirus software (Windows Defender, Norton) may interfere with localhost WebSocket connections. Try adding an exception for port 9867.Check for port conflicts — another application may be using 9867. Use lsof -i :9867 to check.Can't control my radioEnsure Hamlib is installed and rigctld is running with the correct model number (rigctld -l to list supported models).Check serial port permissions. Your user may need to be in the dialout or uucp group.Verify the serial port and baud rate match your radio's settings.HTTPS mixed content warningBrowsers normally block insecure WebSocket (ws://) connections from HTTPS pages. However, connections to localhost and 127.0.0.1 are exempt from this restriction in Chrome, Firefox, and Edge. If you still encounter issues, run Propulse locally on http://localhost or install the Chrome bridge extension.'Error' status in health panelAnother application may be using port 9867. Try a different port:BRIDGE_PORT=9868 npm run devThen update the WebSocket URL in Propulse settings to match.Links & ResourcesHamlib ProjectOpen-source radio control libraryWSJT-XWeak signal digital modes (FT8, FT4, JT65)DX Cluster NodesPublic DX cluster node directoryBridge Source CodePropulse Bridge on GitHub"
`;

exports[`BridgeInfoPage rendered text is unchanged by the #1097 toolkit extraction > matches the frozen baseline for Windows 1`] = `
"BackProPulse BridgeConnect your radio hardware to PropulseDisconnectedws://127.0.0.1:9867Start the bridge server to connectSetup GuideWhat is the Bridge?The ProPulse Bridge is a lightweight server that runs on your computer alongside Propulse.It acts as a secure translator between the web application and your radio equipment — your transceiver, logging software, and digital mode programs.Because it runs only on localhost, your equipment is never exposed to the internet.Communication happens over WebSocket — a real-time, bidirectional protocol that lets Propulse and your radio talk to each other with millisecond latency.CapabilitiesCAT ControlControl your transceiver directly from the browser. Tune to a DX spot with one click, switch modes for digital or CW, activate PTT for transmit.Powered by Hamlib, supporting over 2,000 radio models from Icom, Yaesu, Kenwood, Elecraft, FlexRadio, and more.What this means: Hear a rare DX station on the cluster? One click and your radio is already on frequency.DX Cluster RelayReceive real-time DX spots from the worldwide cluster network directly in Propulse. Spots appear on the PropSphere map and band planner automatically.Connects to standard DX cluster nodes via telnet — works with any public node. No separate telnet client needed.What this means: See who's on the air right now, where they are on the globe, and jump to their frequency instantly.WSJT-X IntegrationSee FT8, FT4, and JT65 decodes from WSJT-X in real-time within Propulse. Auto-log QSOs when WSJT-X reports a completed contact.Listens on WSJT-X's standard UDP multicast port — zero configuration in most setups. Track which callsigns are being decoded on the waterfall.What this means: Your digital mode activity flows seamlessly into your Propulse logbook and map.Multi-Operator SyncCoordinate multiple operators during contest operations from different computers. Frequency locking prevents two operators from transmitting on the same frequency.QSO deconfliction avoids duplicate contacts. Shared notes and session state keep everyone in sync — all in real-time.What this means: Run a multi-op contest station with Propulse as your coordination hub.Live ConnectionDisconnectedTest ConnectionArchitectureThe bridge sits between your browser and hardware, routing messages over localhost.Propulse(Browser)Bridge Serverlocalhost:9867WebSocketHamlibrigctldDX ClusterTelnetWSJT-XUDP 2237Your RadioTransceiverMessage ProtocolAll messages use a JSON envelope format with type, id, timestamp, and payload fields.Security ModelBinds exclusively to localhost (127.0.0.1) — no remote connections acceptedCAT control commands cannot be issued from outside your computerContest data and QSO information never leave your local networkNeed remote access for multi-computer setups?ssh -L 9867:127.0.0.1:9867 user@contest-pcSSH tunnels forward the bridge port securely. Only use this with trusted machines and authenticated SSH keys.Setup GuidePick your platformSteps shown for Windows.WindowsmacOSLinux1Install the BridgeClone or download the Propulse repository, then install dependencies:cd bridge
npm installAlternatively, download the pre-built binary propulse-bridge-x86_64-pc-windows-msvc.exe from GitHub Releases.2Start the ServerDevelopment mode (auto-reloads on changes):npm run devProduction mode:npm run build
npm start3Verify ConnectionLook for the green dot next to System Health in the Propulse header. It should show "Bridge: Connected."You can also use the "Test Connection" button above to verify.4Configure CAT Control (optional)Install Hamlib and start rigctld with your radio's model number and serial port:rigctld -m 3085 -r COM3 -s 384005Configure DX Cluster (optional)Add a cluster node address in the bridge configuration. Common public nodes include:# Example bridge config
cluster_host = "dxc.nc7j.com"
cluster_port = 7373
callsign = "YOUR_CALL"6Configure WSJT-X (optional)In WSJT-X, go to File → Settings → Reporting and enable "Accept UDP requests".The default UDP port is 2237. The bridge listens on this port automatically.TroubleshootingBridge shows 'Disconnected'Confirm the bridge server is running (npm run dev or the standalone binary).Check that port 9867 is not blocked by your firewall.Ensure the WebSocket URL matches (ws://127.0.0.1:9867).Connection keeps droppingSome antivirus software (Windows Defender, Norton) may interfere with localhost WebSocket connections. Try adding an exception for port 9867.Check for port conflicts — another application may be using 9867. Use netstat -ano | findstr 9867 to check.Can't control my radioEnsure Hamlib is installed and rigctld is running with the correct model number (rigctld -l to list supported models).Verify the serial port and baud rate match your radio's settings.HTTPS mixed content warningBrowsers normally block insecure WebSocket (ws://) connections from HTTPS pages. However, connections to localhost and 127.0.0.1 are exempt from this restriction in Chrome, Firefox, and Edge. If you still encounter issues, run Propulse locally on http://localhost or install the Chrome bridge extension.'Error' status in health panelAnother application may be using port 9867. Try a different port:BRIDGE_PORT=9868 npm run devThen update the WebSocket URL in Propulse settings to match.Links & ResourcesHamlib ProjectOpen-source radio control libraryWSJT-XWeak signal digital modes (FT8, FT4, JT65)DX Cluster NodesPublic DX cluster node directoryBridge Source CodePropulse Bridge on GitHub"
`;

