exports[`SetupGuidePage rendered text is unchanged by the #1097 toolkit extraction > matches the frozen baseline for Linux 1`] = `
"BackProPulse SetupGet your station connected in minutes. No programming experience needed — just follow the steps below.Select your platformShowing instructions for Linux.WindowsmacOSLinuxQuick Status DashboardThis shows whether each piece of the system is running. Only the Bridge Server can be tested from the browser — the others require the setup script.Node.jsPowers the bridge serverBridge ServerNot runningHamlibControls your radio via computerOptionalWSJT-XDigital modes (FT8, FT4)OptionalDX ClusterLive spots from other operatorsOptionalTest Bridge ConnectionNot runningHow do I open a terminal?A terminal (also called a command prompt) is a text window where you type commands. Here is how to open one on each platform:Windows: Right-click the Start button and choose "Windows PowerShell" or "Terminal".macOS: Press Cmd + Space, type "Terminal", press Enter.Linux: Right-click the desktop and choose "Open Terminal", or press Ctrl + Alt + T.One-Line InstallerRECOMMENDEDRuns in about 2 minutes.Copy and paste this single command into your terminal (or PowerShell on Windows). It will check what you already have installed, set up anything that's missing, and verify everything works — all automatically.Terminal (Linux)curl -fsSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/crypticpy/propulse/main/scripts/setup.sh | bashOr download and review first:setup.shWe recommend reviewing scripts before running them. View source on GitHub.Manual Setup GuidePrefer to set things up yourself? Expand each step below.Estimated time: ~15 minutes total (Steps 1-2 required, ~7 min. Steps 3-6 optional.)1Install Node.js (required for the bridge) — ~5 minRequiredNode.js is a free program that powers the bridge server. You need version 18 or newer — we recommend the latest LTS release (v22).Using NodeSource (Debian/Ubuntu)curl -fsSL https://deb.nodesource.com/setup_22.x | sudo -E bash -
sudo apt install nodejsOr using nvmcurl -o- https://raw.githubusercontent.com/nvm-sh/nvm/v0.40.1/install.sh | bash
nvm install 22
nvm use 22Verify installationnode -v
# Should show v22.x.x2Install & Start the Bridge — ~2 minRequiredClone or download the Propulse repository, then install the bridge dependencies.cd bridge
npm installDevelopment mode (auto-reloads)npm run devOr from the project rootnpm run bridgeTest ConnectionNot running3Install Hamlib (Optional — lets your computer control your radio) — ~10 minOptionalHamlib is free software that lets Propulse read and control your radio — things like changing frequency, mode, and band from the screen. It supports over 2,000 radio models. You install Hamlib, then start its "rigctld" service pointing at your radio.sudo apt install libhamlib-utilsCommon radio model referenceRadioModel #CommandIcom IC-73003085rigctld -m 3085 -r /dev/ttyUSB0 -s 115200Yaesu FT-991A1035rigctld -m 1035 -r /dev/ttyUSB0 -s 38400Kenwood TS-890S2044rigctld -m 2044 -r /dev/ttyUSB0 -s 115200Elecraft K32036rigctld -m 2036 -r /dev/ttyUSB0 -s 38400FlexRadio 6x002507rigctld -m 2507 -r 127.0.0.1:4992Serial ports on Linux are typically /dev/ttyUSB0 or /dev/ttyACM0.You may need serial port permissions:sudo usermod -aG dialout $USER
# Log out and back in for changes to take effect4Configure WSJT-X (Optional — for FT8/FT4 digital modes) — ~2 minOptionalIf you use WSJT-X for FT8 or FT4, you can connect it to Propulse. Decoded callsigns will appear live, and completed QSOs are automatically logged. WSJT-X sends data to the bridge over your local network (UDP port 2237) — no internet required.1Open WSJT-X SettingsGo to File → Settings → Reporting2Enable UDPCheck "Accept UDP requests"3Set the portEnsure the UDP port is set to 2237 (the default). The bridge auto-listens on this port.Once configured, decoded callsigns from FT8/FT4 sessions will appear in Propulse automatically, and completed QSOs will be auto-logged.5Configure DX Cluster (Optional — live spots from other operators) — ~2 minOptionalA DX Cluster is like a shared bulletin board where ham operators post who they're hearing and on what frequency. Propulse shows these spots on the map in real-time. You configure this in the app — no extra software needed.In Propulse, go to Settings → Connections → DX Cluster.Common cluster nodesNodeRegiondxc.nc7j.com:7373North Americadxc.ve7cc.net:23Canadadxc.hb9drv.ch:8000EuropeYour callsign is required for cluster login. Enter it in Settings.6Verify Everything — ~1 minOptionalUse the button below to test the bridge, then confirm each component is working.Test Bridge ConnectionNot runningVerification checklistBridge server is running and connectedHamlib rigctld started (if using CAT control)WSJT-X UDP reporting enabled (if using FT8/FT4)DX Cluster node configured (if using live spots)System Health →Bridge Details →Architecture OverviewThe bridge sits between your browser and radio hardware, routing all messages over localhost.Propulse(Browser)Bridge Serverlocalhost:9867WebSocketHamlibrigctldDX ClusterTelnetWSJT-XUDP 2237Your RadioTransceiverTroubleshootingNode.js version is too oldThe bridge requires Node.js 18+. Check your version with node -v.To upgrade:Re-run the NodeSource setup script for the latest LTS, or use nvm install 22Bridge won't connectConfirm the bridge server is running (npm run dev in the bridge/ directory).Check that port 9867 is not blocked by your firewall.Verify the WebSocket URL is ws://127.0.0.1:9867.Port 9867 is already in useFind what is using the port:lsof -i :9867Or run the bridge on a different port:BRIDGE_PORT=9868 npm run devThen update the WebSocket URL in Propulse settings to match.Can't control my radioVerify rigctld is running with the correct model number. Use rigctld -l to list all supported models.Check the serial port and baud rate match your radio's CAT settings.Ensure your user is in the dialout group: sudo usermod -aG dialout $USER then log out and back in.WSJT-X decodes not showingIn WSJT-X, verify File → Settings → Reporting → Accept UDP requests is checked.Confirm the UDP port is 2237.Make sure the bridge server is running — it listens for WSJT-X UDP traffic automatically.Permission denied on serial port (Linux)Add your user to the dialout group:sudo usermod -aG dialout $USERYou must log out and log back in (or reboot) for the change to take effect. Verify with groups.Links & ResourcesSetup Scripts (GitHub)Automated installer scripts for all platformsHamlib ProjectOpen-source radio control library (2,000+ models)WSJT-XWeak signal digital modes (FT8, FT4, JT65)DX Cluster InfoPublic DX cluster node directoryProPulse Bridge InfoDetailed bridge capabilities and protocol referenceSystem HealthLive system diagnostics and connection monitoringGlossaryQuick definitions of the technical terms used on this page.Node.jsA free program that runs JavaScript on your computer. It powers the bridge server.npmNode Package Manager — a tool that comes with Node.js for installing software packages.Bridge ServerA small program running on your computer that connects Propulse (in the browser) to your radio hardware.WebSocketA communication channel that keeps a live, two-way connection open between your browser and the bridge so data flows instantly.CAT ControlComputer Aided Transceiver — a standard way for a computer to control a radio (change frequency, mode, etc.).Hamlib / rigctldHamlib is free radio-control software. rigctld is the background service that translates commands for your specific radio model.WSJT-XFree software for digital weak-signal modes like FT8 and FT4. It decodes radio signals and can log contacts automatically.UDPUser Datagram Protocol — a fast network protocol. WSJT-X uses it to send decoded data to the bridge over your local network.DX ClusterA worldwide network where ham radio operators share real-time reports of who they are hearing on the air.Terminal / Command PromptA text-based window where you type commands. Called 'Terminal' on Mac/Linux and 'PowerShell' or 'Command Prompt' on Windows.Serial PortA physical or virtual connection between your computer and radio (usually via USB cable). Named COM3/COM4 on Windows, /dev/ttyUSB0 on Linux.Baud RateThe speed at which data travels over a serial port, measured in bits per second. Must match between your computer and radio (common values: 9600, 38400, 115200)."
`;

